        "entry_key": "MeasuredScopeDiff",
        "anchor_id": {
          "file_path": "track/items/scope-diff-ceiling-admission-enforcement-2026-07-29/spec.json",
          "element_id": "IN-14"
        }
      },
      "reason": "IN-14 moves the source of truth for counting a scope's lines out of workflow prose and into the CLI implementation, and fixes that definition: a scope's line figure is additions plus deletions over the committed, staged and unstaged changes measured from the base, untracked files are counted in full as additions, and scope classification reuses the existing review-scope classification mechanism. MeasuredScopeDiff decides none of that. Its declared surface is new(scope, lines), scope() and lines(): it receives a ScopeName and an already-obtained LineCount, stores them, and hands them back unchanged. It reads no worktree, resolves no base, distinguishes no committed from unstaged change, and classifies no file, so not one of the three counting rules is expressible through it. A test bound here could only pass a number to the constructor and read the same number back, which would show the arrangement of the test rather than the counting definition IN-14 promises. That definition is realised by the adapter that produces the figure — a different entry, which does read the worktree and does classify."
    },
    {
      "kind": "waiver",
      "edge_id": {
        "entry_key": "MeasuredScopeDiff",
        "anchor_id": {
          "file_path": "track/items/scope-diff-ceiling-admission-enforcement-2026-07-29/spec.json",
          "element_id": "AC-17"
        }
      },
      "reason": "AC-17 is checked by observing that the scope line computation exists on the CLI side and that it behaves per the fixed definition: additions plus deletions summed, untracked files counted in full as additions, and the existing review-scope classification mechanism reused. MeasuredScopeDiff is the value that crosses the boundary once such a computation has finished, not the computation. Its declaration — new(scope, lines), scope(), lines() — stores a ScopeName next to a LineCount and returns them unchanged; it holds exactly the same value however the figure was arrived at, so neither the location of the computation nor any of its three counting rules can be observed through it. A test bound to this entry would assert the constructor's storage rather than AC-17's promise. The entry that can exhibit that promise is the one that performs the measurement."
    },
