        "entry_key": "BatchPlanDocument",
        "anchor_id": {
          "file_path": "track/items/scope-diff-ceiling-admission-enforcement-2026-07-29/spec.json",
          "element_id": "IN-01"
        }
      },
      "reason": "IN-01 promises four things: that batch-plan.json is added as the fourth terminal Phase 3 planning artifact, that impl-planner alone writes it, that the admission-deciding CLI only reads it, and that Phase 3 authoring proceeds in the stated dependency order with each file completed in a single write. BatchPlanDocument is the in-memory model of that artifact's content and reaches none of those facts. It touches no file system, so it cannot observe that the artifact exists or where it sits among the Phase 3 outputs. It holds no notion of an author, a capability contract or a writes-forbidden list, so the single-writer restriction is invisible to it. It carries no history of how the value was produced, so the same document results from any authoring order and from any number of writes; nothing in it distinguishes one authoring sequence from another. The one part that might look testable — that consumers only read — is a property of the declaration rather than of behaviour: the type offers a constructor and borrowing accessors (track_id, task_estimates, batches, estimate_for, batch_of, scope_total, current_batch) and no mutator at all, so a consumer that edits a plan through this type is not expressible and no test can tell a read-only consumer from a writing one. Whatever a test asserted here would restate the type system or the absence of file access rather than IN-01's promise; the evidence the anchor asks for lives in the capability contracts, the workflow's authoring sequence and the CLI wiring, none of which this declaration reaches."
    },
